aking the format \"<Concurred/Dissented>, argued that <argument goes here>.\"\n        }\n    ]\n}\n\nFor \"rights_affected\", set each item only to one of [\"protected\",\"infringed\",\"NA\"] depending on how the court's decision\naffected that item as a legal right.\n\nFor individual judge opinions, only set the \"ruling\" to \"other\" when they recuse or have some procedural reason\nnot to concur or dissent. Individual judges may disagree on parts of an argument but ultimately still agree with it.\n\nIf individual opinions for judges are not available, do not include them in\nthe response. If individual opinions for judges are just a ruling without\na unique opinion argument, set the \"description\" to \"<Concurred/Dissented> with majority opinion\".\nEnsure that the individual opinion \"description\" for judges is written in plain, accessible, and brief\nEnglish as much as possible. Avoid excessive legalese.\n\nEnsure that each judge's full name is displayed. Do not infer complete judge\nnames if they are incomplete. Only record judge names exactly as they are\nlisted in this document. Make sure a name only appears once. Given that this\nis one case, make sure your response contains only 1 issue being debated, 1\ncourt decision, and 1 list of individual judge decisions.\n\nHere is the opinion document and dataframe you need to analyze:", "skips": {"case_id": ["122782_Kansas_2025/06/27_78", "20190818_Utah_2024/08/15_313"], "num_skips": 2}, "avg_case_query_time": 15.449669378104877}